  "preamble": "The shift from the bilabial nasal [m] to the alveolar nasal [n] is a common form of place assimilation, particularly in word-final positions. In monosyllabic words and inflectional endings, many languages prefer the more neutral alveolar point of articulation. In Cuzco–Bolivian Quechua, *m became n before a consonant (*kimsa > kinsa 'three').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Place Assimilation, Nasalization",
